    {
      "question": "q-107499",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "25ce375e1287501113721a1a08c8c459eecb56f4091519ca53d25720ae001900",
      "new_sha256": "9765fe8da74a6b3c914d9c700c75034331fa94af8357658c2a7df2eb4b3a59fb",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "q-107499",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\text{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $-1-\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{6}{-1+\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-1-\\text{i})}{(-1+\\text{i})(-1-\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-1-\\text{i})}{(-1)^{2}-{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-1-\\text{i})}{1+1} \\\\  & =3(-1-\\text{i}) \\\\  & =-3-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=-3-3\\text{i},$ the magnitude is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}\u2061(z).$ Since $x=-3$ and $y=-3$ are both negative, $z$ lies in the third quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}\u2061(z) & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061|\\frac{-3}{-3}| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}\u20611 \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=3\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}))\n$$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json",
          "file_sha256": "5ebff0ae9f8c47623e4802f97ee1132bab07a5e5ce80e5d3222ec19159d45b0c"
        }
      ],
