    {
      "question": "q-77159",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "67250857297e0c14e76e1febae2173d8630c2f31f6fc8ee699dea0923ef235a7",
      "new_sha256": "18bcd64a16467617f12226e559b1ddfba703fa8f31067550698be7c622fd7ba5",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [
        {
          "question": "q-77159",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "Note that the position vectors of the points $P,$ $Q$ and $R$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p=\u27e82,4,1\u27e9,\\,q=\u27e83,4,2\u27e9,\\,r=\u27e83,3,1\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $\u25b3PQR$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $\u2220QPR=\\theta .$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964292/assets/3003f0cb329a8f8ef9bc315c70df4773ac1f0c54843dea1208c68bd0f6227bd1.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =\u27e83,4,2\u27e9-\u27e82,4,1\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e81,0,1\u27e9 \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =\u27e83,3,1\u27e9-\u27e82,4,1\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e81,-1,0\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{PR}}{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}||\\overset{\\to }{PR}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 1+0\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}\u2061(\\frac{1}{2})={60}^{\u2218}.\n$$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964292/state.json",
          "file_sha256": "3c138e79e69852f73c5d8d62bcc01cfaeaeb160d75072882928b7f2f20f0df35",
          "basis": 650,
          "verification": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964292/edb-import/verification.json"
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "q-77159",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "Note that the position vectors of the points $P,$ $Q$ and $R$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p=\u27e82,4,1\u27e9,\\,q=\u27e83,4,2\u27e9,\\,r=\u27e83,3,1\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $\u25b3PQR$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $\u2220QPR=\\theta .$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/assets/3003f0cb329a8f8ef9bc315c70df4773ac1f0c54843dea1208c68bd0f6227bd1.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =\u27e83,4,2\u27e9-\u27e82,4,1\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e81,0,1\u27e9 \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =\u27e83,3,1\u27e9-\u27e82,4,1\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e81,-1,0\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{PR}}{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}||\\overset{\\to }{PR}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 1+0\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}\u2061(\\frac{1}{2})={60}^{\u2218}.\n$$",
